one of the big mistakes that i think uh  uh companies make is they think of the  sales goals as the end-all be-all and  they have no higher purpose no higher uh  contribution other than the goal itself  um and so we overuse um uh sort of uh  war and sports analogies to to hit that  goal right it's all very finite thinking  as if it's the end  i like to think of business much more  like a lifestyle um it's much more like  being healthy right the purpose is to be  healthy for example and there are many  things we have to do to be healthy we  have to eat right we have to exercise we  have to get enough sleep uh we have to  nurse our personal relationships there's  probably 30 other things as well it's  the same in business we have to have  good marketing we have good leadership  good product good you know good sales  for us all of these things and it's hard  to maintain all of those things at very  high level all the time they sort of wax  and wane the opportunity is to work hard  it's it's an infinite game to keep those  things um going all the time and it is  perfectly acceptable to set arbitrary  goals  within  uh within that context now most sales  goals are arbitrary somebody sat in an  office and said we're going to hit this  number by this date and so we all went  and did it right which like if you want  to live a healthy lifestyle you say i  want to lose x amount of weight by x  date and you do all the things you have  to do to lose that weight you exercise  you eat right you weigh yourself every  day you met you you count the metrics  metrics are very very healthy people  like metrics if we think about running a  marathon  without mile markers it's actually  unnerving we need to be able to count  our progress to feel like we're moving  forward it's a basic human need and so  we count  we look down at the scale every day some  days we feel good some days we feel bad  and we hit our goal on the right day we  feel amazing like hitting a sales goal  but here's the problem you have to keep  exercising eating and eating right for  the rest of your life you don't get to  stop doing those things and and the  problem in business is we we treat that  goal like it's the end except we have to  keep working on all those things the  good leadership the good sales training  the good culture it has to continue what  i think is more interesting than hitting  the goal  is what happens if you miss the goal  so if you look down at that scale and  you miss your goal you miss the  arbitrary number by that arbitrary date  you know what happens  nothing  nothing happens in fact because you've  been working hard you're actually much  healthier now than where you were  started you just didn't predict the  right day and the right time  and i think what's very very important  if we try and contextualize goal setting  within a purpose-driven company  is to recognize that those numbers and  those dates are not absolute  and if you you look at the trend data i  think it's really important to look at  trend data because there are many teams  who you know their sales are up their  sales are down throughout the year it's  like a roller coaster they have a big  promotion and a big sales push right at  the end of the year they hit their goal  and for some reason we think that that  was good  but if you look at another team where  everything is really steady and really  good morale is high  and and and and things you know but they  just happen to miss the goal but clearly  they're going to hit it two months after  clearly  we give that team nothing  and so i think it's okay to have the  goals it's good to count things but i  think what's what as we move forward and  become more purpose-driven  infinite-minded companies we have to  start including trend data to understand  that it's contextualized like this goal  is designed to advance something bigger  than ourselves
 

